--- !u!114 &7693301045479975054
=======
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 456c7c2b477e4253b9e4faf9ed4b18a9, type: 3}
  m_Name: InstructionsParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.InstructionsParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Title:
    m_Untranslated: INSTRUCTIONS
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "<b>1</b>.  Select the <b>Add (+)</b> button in the upper-left corner of the <b>Hierarchy</b> window, then select <b>3D Object</b> > <b>Cube</b>.\n\n<b>2</b>.  With the <b>Scale</b> tool, turn the cube into a platform the robot can stand on.\n\n<b>3</b>.  Add a material to the <b>Cube</b> GameObject (<b>Assets</b> > <b>Materials</b>).\n\n<b>4</b>.  In the <b>Hierarchy</b> window, right click the <b>Cube (1)</b> GameObject and select <b>Duplicate</b> or press <b>Ctrl</b>+<b>D</b> (macOS: <b>Cmd</b>+<b>D</b>). \n\n<b>5</b>.  Select the newly created duplicate GameObject and use the <b>Move</b> tool to position it up and towards the star.\n\n<b>6</b>.  Repeat steps 4 and 5 until you think you've created a set of stairs the robot can use to reach the star.\n\n<b>7</b>.  Enter Play mode and test your design.\n\n<b>Note</b>: You might need to experiment to get the position of these platforms just right. Game designers iterate many times on their designs; they change things, then test, then change more, and then test more. This process is called <b>level design,</b> a fundamental part of game development."
  m_CriteriaCompletion: 0
  m_Criteria:
    m_Items: []
